#!/usr/bin/env xetal
# HEX1000 reading keys: a program that waits for a key on K, stores it
# in the next RAM word, and waits for the key to be released. The keys
# C, A, F, E (K 12, 10, 15, 14) are pressed in turn, each held for 8
# instructions with 9 instructions of no key (K 0) between, and RAM
# file 0 ends up holding 12 10 15 14. K 0 means no key, so a key whose
# code is 0 cannot be read this way; the calculator's keyboard is a
# matrix scanned through the R latches (docs/machine.md, Keys).
# Run with "just demo-lib Hex1000 keys".
"hx:" u_se< "Hex1000"
scan := hx:a_ssemble "wait: KNEZ   ; S: a key is down\nBR got\nBR wait\ngot: TKA    ; the key into A\nTAMIY        ; into RAM, Y + 1\nup: KNEZ     ; wait for the release\nBR up\nBR wait"
0 hx:l_isting scan
presses := 8 2 r_eshape 12 8 0 9 10 8 0 9 15 8 0 9 14 8 0 9
"K held" hx:t_able presses
after := scan hx:r_unKeys (hx:Reset, presses)
hx:RegNames hx:t_able hx:r_egs after
"w0 w1 w2 w3 w4 w5 w6 w7" hx:t_able 8 t_ake f_irst hx:r_am after
